   "file": "plugins/specclaw/references/full/build.md",
   "fp": "21be20b2f37d",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 164,
   "message": "Verify Step 3.52 deletes any existing `security-report.md` before it spawns the agent, so a stale report cannot pass as a fresh one. The other two call sites do not do this (B1). Build step 2c does not delete `security/<TASK_ID>.md` before it spawns the agent. Plan step 3c (plan.md:27) does not delete `security-requirements.md` either. Both then check only that the file exists and, for build, that it has a `**Verdict:**` line. On a rebuild or a re-plan where the agent errors without writing, the old file passes that check. An unreviewed task then reads as reviewed, or out-of-date requirements read as current. Fix: delete the target file before spawning, in both build 2c and plan 3c, as verify does.",
   "original_severity": "major",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B3",
   "scenario": "T4 is built and `security/T4.md` gets `**Verdict:** APPROVED`. T4 is retried with a new diff and the infosec-reviewer call errors without writing. `security/T4.md` still exists with a Verdict line, so no NOT_RUN is written. Verify then reads T4 as APPROVED even though the new code was never reviewed.",
   "severity": "minor",
   "unclassified": false
  },
  {
   "class": "other",
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/presets/react-spa.md",
   "fp": "baabbdaf1a90",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 29,
   "message": "The preset and review-pack copies of the same rules disagree. The `Tokens` rule bans only `localStorage`, but RS7 also bans `sessionStorage`. The `Links` rule bans every user-supplied `href`, while RS10 allows one once the scheme is checked. Builders get only the preset, so a builder can follow it and still trip RS7. Align the preset wording with RS7 and RS10.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B4",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor",
   "unclassified": false
  },
  {
   "class": "other",
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/tests/evals/infosec/xff-rate-limit/fixed.js",
   "fp": "73010163c411",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 5,
   "message": "The 'fixed' sample keys the limiter on the compound `email:ip`. That is neither an account limit nor an IP limit. An attacker who rotates IPs gets a fresh budget against one account. The Proxy rule asks for account *as well as* IP, so a correct agent may raise a WARN here. Use two limiters, one keyed on the account and one on `req.ip`.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B6",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor",
   "unclassified": false
  },
  {
   "class": "other",
   "file": ".github/workflows/infosec-evals.yml",
   "fp": "15fdac9676a8",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 26,
   "message": "`timeout-minutes: 60` is far below the worst case of 8 cases \u00d7 2 samples \u00d7 3 reps \u00d7 300 s per call. If the job is killed mid-run, the uploaded result file has the table rows but no rate lines. Either raise the job timeout above the reps \u00d7 cases \u00d7 call-timeout budget, or lower the per-call timeout.",
